WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.499 --> 00:00:02.220
YUFENG: On this episode
of AI Adventures,

00:00:02.220 --> 00:00:04.553
we will attempt to go through
an entire machine learning

00:00:04.553 --> 00:00:07.170
workflow in one video,
pulling best practices

00:00:07.170 --> 00:00:08.820
from our previous episodes.

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:11.864
It's a bit of material,
but I think we can do it.

00:00:11.864 --> 00:00:13.530
Training a model with
the MNIST data set

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:16.600
is often considered the Hello
World of machine learning.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:18.870
But that's been done
many times over.

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:22.140
And unfortunately, just because
a model does well on MNIST,

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:24.660
it's not necessarily
predictive of high performance

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:27.780
with other data sets,
especially since most image data

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:31.500
we have today are more complex
than handwritten digits.

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:34.410
Zalando decided it was time
to make MNIST fashionable

00:00:34.410 --> 00:00:37.970
again and recently released a
data set called Fashion-MNIST.

00:00:37.970 --> 00:00:40.560
It's the exact same format
as the regular MNIST,

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:44.520
except the data is in the form
of pictures of various clothing

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:47.070
types, shoes, bags.

00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:48.960
It's still across 10
categories, though.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:52.210
And the images are
still 28-by-28 pixels.

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:55.290
So let's train a model to
detect which type of clothing

00:00:55.290 --> 00:00:56.820
is being shown.

00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:59.640
We'll start by building a
linear classifier, as usual,

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:01.390
and see how we do.

00:01:01.390 --> 00:01:03.660
And we'll use TensorFlow's
Estimator Framework

00:01:03.660 --> 00:01:06.630
to make our code easy to
write and easy to maintain.

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:09.672
As a reminder, we'll
first load in the data,

00:01:09.672 --> 00:01:11.130
create our classifier,
and then run

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:12.370
the training and evaluation.

00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:15.910
We'll also make some predictions
directly from our local model.

00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:17.490
Let's start by
creating our model.

00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:19.110
We'll flatten that
data set from being

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:23.580
28-by-28 to 1-by-784
pixels and make a feature

00:01:23.580 --> 00:01:25.560
column called "pixels."

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:27.240
This is analogous to
our flower features

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:30.220
from Episode 3, Plain
and Simple Estimators.

00:01:30.220 --> 00:01:32.610
Next, we'll create
our linear classifier.

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:34.530
We can have 10 different
possible classes

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:36.840
to label, instead of the
three that we used previously

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:38.610
with the iris flowers.

00:01:38.610 --> 00:01:41.160
To run our training, we'll
need to set up our data set

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:42.510
and input function.

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:46.050
TensorFlow has a built-in
utility to accept a NumPy array

00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:48.550
and generate an input
function right from that.

00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:50.640
So let's take advantage of it.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:53.610
We'll load in our data set
using the input data module.

00:01:53.610 --> 00:01:55.770
I've already downloaded
the data set to a folder,

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:57.930
so we'll point to that here.

00:01:57.930 --> 00:02:01.020
Now we can call classifier.train
to bring together

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:05.310
our classifier, the input
function, and the data set.

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:09.479
Finally, we run an evaluation
step, to see how our model did.

00:02:09.479 --> 00:02:11.470
When we use the
classic MNIST data set,

00:02:11.470 --> 00:02:15.820
this linear model typically
gets about 91% accuracy.

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:19.075
However, Fashion MNIST is a
considerably more complex data

00:02:19.075 --> 00:02:20.920
set and we can
only really achieve

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:25.210
an accuracy in the low 80's, and
sometimes even lower than that.

00:02:25.210 --> 00:02:27.360
So how can we do better?

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:30.840
As we learned in Episode
6, let's go deep.

00:02:30.840 --> 00:02:34.110
Swapping in the DNN classifier
is a one-line change.

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:37.050
And we can now rerun our
training and evaluation

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:39.450
to see if our deep neural
network can conform

00:02:39.450 --> 00:02:41.670
any better than the linear one.

00:02:41.670 --> 00:02:43.705
And as we discussed
in Episode 5,

00:02:43.705 --> 00:02:45.330
we should bring up
tensor board to take

00:02:45.330 --> 00:02:48.950
a look at these two models'
performance side by side.

00:02:48.950 --> 00:02:51.620
It looks like the deep network
could definitely use some more

00:02:51.620 --> 00:02:52.940
time to train, though.

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:54.810
Estimators makes this easy.

00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:57.830
All we need to do is rerun the
call to the Train and Evaluate

00:02:57.830 --> 00:02:59.606
functions.

00:02:59.606 --> 00:03:00.980
Looking at tensor
board, it seems

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:03.110
like my deep model
is performing no

00:03:03.110 --> 00:03:05.510
better than my linear one did.

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:08.030
This is perhaps an
opportunity, however,

00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:10.250
to tune some of my
hyper parameters,

00:03:10.250 --> 00:03:12.440
like we talked
about in Episode 2.

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:14.630
Maybe my model
needs to be larger

00:03:14.630 --> 00:03:16.890
to accommodate the
complexity of this data set,

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:19.520
or perhaps my learning
rate needs to be lowered.

00:03:19.520 --> 00:03:21.620
Experimenting with
these parameters a bit,

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:23.570
we can finally
breakthrough and achieve

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:27.260
a higher overall accuracy than
our linear model can obtain.

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:28.820
It takes quite a
bit more training,

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:31.070
but ultimately this
is worth it to achieve

00:03:31.070 --> 00:03:33.670
those higher-accuracy numbers.

00:03:33.670 --> 00:03:36.310
Notice also that the
linear model plateaus

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:37.900
earlier than the deep network.

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:41.020
Because deep networks are often
more complex than linear ones,

00:03:41.020 --> 00:03:43.100
they can take longer to train.

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:46.570
And at this stage, say
we're happy with our model,

00:03:46.570 --> 00:03:50.230
we'd be able to export it and
produce a scalable Fashion

00:03:50.230 --> 00:03:51.970
MNIST classifier API.

00:03:51.970 --> 00:03:55.380
You can see Episode 4 for more
details on how to do that.

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:57.610
Let's also take a quick
peek at how you can make

00:03:57.610 --> 00:03:59.980
predictions using estimators.

00:03:59.980 --> 00:04:01.750
In large part, it
looks just like how

00:04:01.750 --> 00:04:03.310
we called Train and Evaluate.

00:04:03.310 --> 00:04:05.410
That's one of the great
things about Estimators,

00:04:05.410 --> 00:04:07.000
the consistent interface.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:10.510
Notice that this time, we've
specified a batch size of 1,

00:04:10.510 --> 00:04:14.350
num_epochs of 1, and shuffle as
False This is because we want

00:04:14.350 --> 00:04:16.880
the predictions
to go one by one,

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:19.300
making predictions
through all the data

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:21.250
and preserving that order.

00:04:21.250 --> 00:04:23.050
I've extracted five
images from the middle

00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:26.276
of the valuation data set for
us to try some predictions on.

00:04:26.276 --> 00:04:28.150
And I picked these five
not just because they

00:04:28.150 --> 00:04:30.400
were in the middle, but
because my model managed

00:04:30.400 --> 00:04:32.170
to get two of them wrong.

00:04:32.170 --> 00:04:34.510
Both were supposed to be shirts.

00:04:34.510 --> 00:04:37.210
But the model thought that
the third example was a bag

00:04:37.210 --> 00:04:39.820
and the fifth example
was a coat, incorrectly.

00:04:39.820 --> 00:04:42.254
And you can see, looking
at these images, how

00:04:42.254 --> 00:04:44.420
these examples are more
challenging than handwritten

00:04:44.420 --> 00:04:46.360
numbers, if for no
other reason than just

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:48.920
the graininess of the images.

00:04:48.920 --> 00:04:50.420
So how did your model perform?

00:04:50.420 --> 00:04:51.920
And what parameters
did you end up

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:53.840
using to achieve that accuracy?

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:56.402
Let me know below
in the comments.

00:04:56.402 --> 00:04:58.610
You can find the code that
I used to train this model

00:04:58.610 --> 00:05:01.430
and generate these images,
also in the links below,

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:03.290
along with more links
to the other resources

00:05:03.290 --> 00:05:05.150
we talked about in this episode.

00:05:05.150 --> 00:05:06.740
Our next set of
videos will be focused

00:05:06.740 --> 00:05:09.320
on some of the tools of the
machine learning ecosystem,

00:05:09.320 --> 00:05:11.660
to help you build out your
workflow and tool chain,

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:14.060
as well as showcase even
more architectures that you

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:16.760
can employ to solve your
machine learning problems.

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:18.350
I look forward to
seeing you there.

00:05:18.350 --> 00:05:21.690
And until then, keep
on machine learning.

00:05:21.690 --> 00:05:24.320
Thanks for watching this
episode of Cloud AI Adventures.

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:25.880
Be sure to subscribe
to the channel

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:29.290
to catch future episodes
right when they come out.

